{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>That Your foot may crush</b> — כף רגלך (the sole of Your foot) directly against David, for You ascended on high." "<b>And the tav of</b> תמחץ רגלך — is feminine, like רגלי עמדה במישור, and the word is from the stative verbs. Or the tav of תמחץ is directly against David, and this is by way of parable. And R. Judah said: כי תמחץ is inverted, like חמוץ בגדים." "<b>From your dogs' tongue</b> — an allusion to the enemies who used to speak against David." "<b>From enemies</b> — from the blood of enemies." "<b>From it</b> — like מן הוא והלאה, and the munach comes unusually because it is the end of a verse. And some say: from the general body of enemies." "<b>And from it</b> — like שמץ מנהו, an allusion to the king. And others said: כי מנהו, like צוהו, and the three vowels are not found under a kamatz gadol, for it is a small patach."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 68, :notes {1 "The comparison to חמוץ בגדים (Zephaniah 1:8) is retained literally; the nature of the inversion is not explained by Ibn Ezra.", 5 "שמץ מנהו retained as cited; the allusion is left cryptic per discipline. The three-vowel reference is obscure; translated hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:33:16.280789470Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}